content.explanation:
We just covered absolute error—the raw gap between measured and true. But raw gaps can be misleading on their own, because they don't account for scale. That's where relative error comes in: {{viz:formula-01}}

You just take the absolute error and divide it by the true value. That single division does something powerful: it turns your error into a fraction of the actual size of what you're measuring, so you can finally compare errors across totally different measurements. {{viz:anim-01}}

Let's run the paperclip example: true length 2 cm, measured 1 cm off → absolute error 1 cm. Relative error = 1/2 = 0.5. That's a massive 50% of the paperclip's length!

Now the running track: true length 100 m, measured 1 cm (0.01 m) off → relative error = 0.01/100 = 0.0001. Practically nothing.

Same absolute error size (well, close), completely different story once you look at it relatively. {{viz:3d-01}}

See how relative error changes as the true value gets bigger while the absolute error stays fixed. {{viz:chart-01}}

Once this clicks, you'll never trust a raw error number again without asking "relative to what?" {{viz:text-01}} Try adjusting the numbers yourself. {{viz:simulation-01}}
